#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310391 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "337be080-59df-51bf-b0d5-14dba139b9c0" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Horizontal Parabola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b885b18-3e83-562e-aa15-bede595287dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-4=2t,\\,\\,y-4={t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310393} {:answer/id #uuid "65732b42-6809-5a6d-8d75-341d87c88a0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-4={t}^{2},\\,\\,y-4=2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310394} {:answer/id #uuid "eaf074c0-d547-57da-90fc-af45742a3986" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-4=2{t}^{2},\\,\\,y-4=4t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310395} {:answer/id #uuid "5d878cd8-ea9d-571e-b2c4-2747cdeb4e68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+4=2t,\\,\\,y+4={t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310396} {:answer/id #uuid "5aebc749-f346-5867-826e-d05adad1e8d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+4={t}^{2},\\,\\,y+4=2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310397}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "65732b42-6809-5a6d-8d75-341d87c88a0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-4={t}^{2},\\,\\,y-4=2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310394} :answer-field/id #uuid "1938660b-7d05-579b-9d0b-397ac95ff0de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310392}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "14e2d3b4-1c1a-54b9-9aa2-2d7f1398ec81" :question/math-academy-id "q-106461" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068270/assets/e5c7f025b6f12599a4ecb5fa0cc9e657fd0b67deb1e50231673113f337ca7776.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the parabola is horizontal, has its vertex at $(4,4),$ and passes through the point $(8,8).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a horizontal parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=p{t}^{2},\\,y-k=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(4,4),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx-4=p{t}^{2},\\,y-4=2pt.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(8,8),$ we can substitute $x=8$ and $y=8$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4=p{t}^{2} \\\\ 4=2pt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =2pt \\\\ 2 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{2}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =p{(\\frac{2}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 4 & =p\\cdot \\frac{4}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 4 & =\\frac{4}{p} \\\\ p & =\\frac{4}{4} \\\\ p & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx-4=(1){t}^{2},\\,y-4=2(1)t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx-4={t}^{2},\\,y-4=2t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357190 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "14079b03-66c0-5657-8909-a7d5ea35bfc0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of Parabola Given Its Parametric Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f0ce42e4-a7d5-57da-b3dd-eb27f70938d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357192}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f0ce42e4-a7d5-57da-b3dd-eb27f70938d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357192} :answer-field/id #uuid "767456eb-e8d8-5700-beb7-2079e41ff6b7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357191} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40743546-cb8e-5497-97a4-a66504271cca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357194}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "40743546-cb8e-5497-97a4-a66504271cca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357194} :answer-field/id #uuid "73ea54b2-2395-5bdf-9780-650ffe63c4ca" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357193}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f54dfea7-1ce0-53bf-b599-41fdc4b47216" :question/math-academy-id "q-323209" :question/problem "Consider the following parametric equation of the parabola\n\n$$\nx+7=-3t,\\,y-2=-9{t}^{2},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this parabola is\n\n$(x\\,+$\n{{field-1}}$)^{2}=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =-\\frac{x+7}{3},\\,{t}^{2}=-\\frac{1}{9}(y-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(-\\frac{x+7}{3})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+7)^{2}}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=-\\frac{1}{9}(y-2)$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+7)^{2}}{9} & =-\\frac{1}{9}(y-2) \\\\ {(x+7)}^{2} & =-(y-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310419 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "337be080-59df-51bf-b0d5-14dba139b9c0" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Horizontal Parabola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f5cfbb25-241e-55fa-a7d4-89ae164d5aaf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+3=-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2},\\,y+1=-t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310421} {:answer/id #uuid "668b71eb-4d27-5add-b663-e98d9703dc8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-3=-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2},\\,y-1=-\\frac{1}{2}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310422} {:answer/id #uuid "6daa4abe-4ca9-5198-82b7-01d76b078024" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+3=-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2},\\,y+1=-\\frac{1}{2}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310423} {:answer/id #uuid "9e016e23-f067-5f42-adf8-de94c24af360" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+3=-2{t}^{2},\\,y+1=-4t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310424} {:answer/id #uuid "a0dc9526-9759-5a8d-99ba-f52ca3e1c07f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-3=-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2},\\,y-1=-t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310425}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f5cfbb25-241e-55fa-a7d4-89ae164d5aaf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+3=-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2},\\,y+1=-t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310421} :answer-field/id #uuid "88f24eaa-bd31-5c6c-80a0-5608034660ab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310420}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "81c9b11f-76b1-5474-bb7c-bc45490a8350" :question/math-academy-id "q-84842" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068270/assets/7cb71517fe60c9d15b6cacddbf5f91c2e4a60103b8917a4d9baa287517a5fd54.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the parabola is horizontal, has its vertex at $(-3,-1),$ and passes through the point $(-5,1).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a horizontal parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=p{t}^{2},\\,y-k=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(-3,-1),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx+3=p{t}^{2},\\,y+1=2pt.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(-5,1),$ we can substitute $x=-5$ and $y=1$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-2=p{t}^{2} \\\\ 2=2pt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =2pt \\\\ 1 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2 & =p{(\\frac{1}{p})}^{2} \\\\ -2 & =p\\cdot \\frac{1}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ -2 & =\\frac{1}{p} \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx+3=(-\\frac{1}{2}){t}^{2},\\,y+1=2(-\\frac{1}{2})t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx+3=-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2},\\,y+1=-t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357195 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "14079b03-66c0-5657-8909-a7d5ea35bfc0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of Parabola Given Its Parametric Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17deb370-813d-50be-9716-9ae480c7204f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+3)^{2}=2(y-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357197} {:answer/id #uuid "2ca7dcfb-c2d3-5599-86dc-6440da954b4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+3)^{2}=8(y-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357198} {:answer/id #uuid "8f148064-a42c-5efa-88b4-d1afc3b0c08a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-3)^{2}=4(y+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357199} {:answer/id #uuid "e3448d61-2644-5b83-99b5-283134c1f834" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-3)^{2}=2(y+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357200} {:answer/id #uuid "2ef1d5fe-77b9-5ed1-a30c-8800c099b320" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+3)^{2}=4(y-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357201}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2ef1d5fe-77b9-5ed1-a30c-8800c099b320" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+3)^{2}=4(y-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357201} :answer-field/id #uuid "01dc42f8-4165-5aab-a5d5-04c40b30a64f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357196}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "20d8f158-c599-5a82-bf4d-bc71d1b096d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-84851" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx+3=2t,\\,y-2={t}^{2},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =\\frac{x+3}{2},\\,{t}^{2}=y-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(\\frac{x+3}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=y-2$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{4} & =y-2 \\\\ (x+3)^{2} & =4(y-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357202 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "14079b03-66c0-5657-8909-a7d5ea35bfc0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of Parabola Given Its Parametric Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5dc5b44c-bfa3-5013-89d4-9d171b8da5ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+1)^{2}=-5(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357204} {:answer/id #uuid "c6ac3a23-451c-51a3-b7f5-2d71382fff55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-1)^{2}=-5(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357205} {:answer/id #uuid "8b2f06e1-c48d-5a6c-8c3c-a48f81a307d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-1)^{2}=-\\frac{5}{2}(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357206} {:answer/id #uuid "7b677554-c650-5f66-87b7-f277e1f8341d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+1)^{2}=-\\frac{5}{2}(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357207} {:answer/id #uuid "e8367f2a-1f38-5156-bfb9-8fa82ab9b0ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-1)^{2}=\\frac{5}{2}(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357208}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c6ac3a23-451c-51a3-b7f5-2d71382fff55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-1)^{2}=-5(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357205} :answer-field/id #uuid "23128741-098a-5682-8d60-27c3890fe51d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357203}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "581a44da-b9bd-54cf-936c-6f38a54486c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-84859" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx+1=-\\frac{5}{4}{t}^{2},\\,y-1=-\\frac{5}{2}t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =-\\frac{4(x+1)}{5},\\,t=-\\frac{2(y-1)}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(-\\frac{2(y-1)}{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4(y-1)^{2}}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=-\\frac{4(x+1)}{5}$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4(y-1)^{2}}{25} & =-\\frac{4(x+1)}{5} \\\\ (y-1)^{2} & =\\frac{25}{4}\\cdot (-\\frac{4(x+1)}{5}) \\\\ (y-1)^{2} & =-5(x+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
